cleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/05/magazine/05standingrock1/05standingrock1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/magazine/05standingrock1/05standingrock1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/magazine/05standingrock1/05standingrock1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 998, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Do You Think Teenagers Can Make a Difference in the World?', 'kicker': 'Student Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-02-02T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caroline Crosson Gilpin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caroline', 'middlename': 'Crosson', 'lastname': 'Gilpin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9782a91b-2453-5642-b5af-35fc4e03bcf2', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9782a91b-2453-5642-b5af-35fc4e03bcf2'}, {'abstract': 'This dish of savory, sweet-tart tomato sauce folded around soft-scrambled eggs is a taste of home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/magazine/the-stir-fried-tomatoes-and-eggs-my-chinese-mother-made.html', 'snippet': 'This dish of savory, sweet-tart tomato sauce folded around soft-scrambled eggs is a taste of home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past two years of writing this column about immigrants and their food, I’ve cooked with a Filipina nurse who used scalpels to debone chicken; a Senegalese family that eats out of the same dish to emphasize everyone’s responsibility to one another; a Mexican Popsicle maker who tried to heal the ache of her divorce by sharing literal sweetness. There was a Slovakian pierogi master whose last act before leaving for the United States was opening the barn doors to set her animals free; a Polish vegetarian who learned to make bigos, a meatfest of a stew, just so she could share her mother’s recipe; and a Palestinian family that lives the American dream by hosting a Thanksgiving every night.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/26/magazine/05eat-copy/05mag-05eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2020/07/26/magazine/05eat-copy/05mag-05eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/26/magazine/05eat-copy/05mag-05eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/26/magazine/05eat-copy/05mag-05eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/26/magazine/05eat-copy/05eat-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2020/07/26/magazine/05eat-copy/05eat-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2020/07/26/magazine/05eat-copy/05eat-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stir-Fried Tomatoes and Eggs My Chinese Mother Made', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What My Chinese Mother Made', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chinese-Americans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-02T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis Lam', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lam', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b754a766-580b-5c52-986e-ae20e64d23f2', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b754a766-580b-5c52-986e-ae20e64d23f2'}, {'abstract': 'Colleges are turning to predictive analytics to pinpoint hotspots for failure — say, a C in English comp, a B in a foundational course in your major.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/education/edlife/will-you-graduate-ask-big-data.html', 'snippet': 'Colleges are turning to predictive analytics to pinpoint hotspots for failure — say, a C in English comp, a B in a foundational course in your major.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At Georgia State’s nursing school, the faculty used to believe that students who got a poor grade in “Conceptual Foundations of Nursing” probably wouldn’t go on to graduation. So they were surprised, after an analysis of student records stretching back a decade, to discover what really made a difference for nursing students: their performance in introductory math.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/education/05DATA/05DATA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/05/education/05DATA/05DATA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 526}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/education/05DATA/05DATA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/education/05DATA/05DATA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1121, 'width': 1279, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/education/05DATA/05DATA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/05/education/05DATA/05DATA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/education/05DATA/05DATA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Will You Graduate? Ask Big Data', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grades That Foretell Your Future', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graduation Rates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-02T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph B. Treaster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd12da78-2cfc-58b9-bc1b-072944143795', 'word_count': 2177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd12da78-2cfc-58b9-bc1b-072944143795'}, {'abstract': 'They encompass nearly all of human history and American identity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-seed-catalogs.html', 'snippet': 'They encompass nearly all of human history and American identity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The growing season never ends here in Los Angeles. Even in the depths of winter, I can read about, obsess over, order and plant new seeds all within a matter of weeks. Because that’s when the seed catalogs arrive, stuffed into mailboxes around the solstice, hitting like a blast of warm air: lists of many hundreds of different fruits, vegetables and flowers. As the lengthening days start tipping toward spring, I pore over these catalogs, reading them cover to cover: There are multihued carrots and beets, cucumbers shaded yellow, orange and rust red; there’s lettuce that’s harvested, curiously, for its stems, and rutabagas that grow to be the size of small children. It is the closest I get in my adult life to the feeling of reading the Sharper Image catalog as a 10-year-old.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/magazine/05lor/05mag-05lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/05/m