,” Jeannette Catsoulis writes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/movies/eloise-review-eliza-dushku.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Legato’s horror movie is “a strange mix of the recycled and the surreal,” Jeannette Catsoulis writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Life is tough for skilled if unremarkable actresses like Eliza Dushku, who, despite early success, can be left foraging among fair-to-middling television series and forgettable movies. Even so, her career dips to a new low with “Eloise,” a horror movie of such ineptitude that it invites sympathy for even its least gifted participants.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Eloise’ Has the Usual Haunted-Hospital Clichés', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eloise', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Eloise (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Legato, Robert (1956- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-02T23:43:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f32e11fa-9121-5f82-a8ef-2f84f2e04f59', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f32e11fa-9121-5f82-a8ef-2f84f2e04f59'}, {'abstract': 'This fictional film by Tim Sutton was inspired by the massacre in 2012 at a showing of the film “The Dark Night Rises” in an Aurora, Colo., theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/movies/dark-night-review.html', 'snippet': 'This fictional film by Tim Sutton was inspired by the massacre in 2012 at a showing of the film “The Dark Night Rises” in an Aurora, Colo., theater.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Dark Night,” written and directed by Tim Sutton, is a movie about a massacre at a movie theater. It was inspired by, and explicitly refers to, the fatal shootings committed by James Holmes at an Aurora, Colo., showing of the film “The Dark Knight Rises.” The wordplay of the title is sufficiently egregious that a potential viewer is apt to think, “Well, if it got a distributor, it has to be better than that.” It is not.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Dark Night,’ Another Movie Theater, Another Shooting', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dark Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dark Night (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sutton, Tim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-02T23:48:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Kenny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c11eaef2-0455-5f88-8cdc-aa784815e356', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c11eaef2-0455-5f88-8cdc-aa784815e356'}, {'abstract': 'This film from Jamal Joseph and Daniel Beaty centers on the struggles of an ex-con re-entering society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/movies/chapter-verse-review.html', 'snippet': 'This film from Jamal Joseph and Daniel Beaty centers on the struggles of an ex-con re-entering society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the way the scene is framed, viewers can tell the beginning of this powerful movie is in fact its tragic-seeming end. On a rainy night in Harlem, a muscular man sits on a milk cart, smoking a cigarette, waiting. What he’s waiting for, a police car, is not long in coming. In voice-over, the man introduces himself as L, adding: “Check with your crew. Find out who I am.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03CHAPTERVERSE/03CHAPTERVERSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03CHAPTERVERSE/03CHAPTERVERSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03CHAPTERVERSE/03CHAPTERVERSE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03CHAPTERVERSE/03CHAPTERVERSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 256, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03CHAPTERVERSE/03CHAPTERVERSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 256}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03CHAPTERVERSE/03CHAPTERVERSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03CHAPTERVERSE/03CHAPTERVERSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 873, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘Chapter & Verse’ Tells of Prison and Redemption', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chapter &amp; Verse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Chapter and Verse (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joseph, Jamal E (1953- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beaty, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-02T23:51:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Kenny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac62210a-44cd-511c-a525-ee00c1b134e9', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac62210a-44cd-511c-a525-ee00c1b134e9'}, {'abstract': 'Barbara Kopple’s film documents how Gigi Gorgeous, formerly Gregory Allan Lazzarato, took control of her transgender destiny with the support of her family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/02/movies/this-is-everything-gigi-gorgeous-review.html', 'snippet': 'Barbara Kopple’s film documents how Gigi Gorgeous, formerly Gregory Allan Lazzarato, took control of her transgender destiny with the support of her family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gigi Lazzarato is very much a self-generated celebrity of the 21st-century vintage. That sounds like a possibly bad thing, and one of the achievements of “This Is Everything: Gigi Gorgeous” is its very entertaining demonstration that it’s not — at least not necessarily.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/03/arts/03THISISEVERY/03THISISEVERY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStan