ction_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d48ac794-f10b-57f2-a213-72e9c539b2f5', 'word_count': 1257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d48ac794-f10b-57f2-a213-72e9c539b2f5'}, {'abstract': 'In his architectural thriller, new at No. 5 in hardcover fiction, Delaney explores the “weird and deeply obsessive” psychology of minimalism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/03/books/review/behind-the-best-sellers-girl-before-author-jp-delaney-on-pseudonyms-and-the-limits-of-marie-kondo.html', 'snippet': 'In his architectural thriller, new at No. 5 in hardcover fiction, Delaney explores the “weird and deeply obsessive” psychology of minimalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Life-Changing Terror of Tidying Up: “He may be an insufferable, affected, narcissistic creep, but he’s also a genius.” That’s how Chip Kidd summed up the architect villain of “Death by Design,” his 2012 Batman story with Dave Taylor, in what’s surely a nod to all the insufferable, affected, narcissistic architects in literary history — even the heroes: Howard Roark, anyone? Certainly the description fits Edward Monkford, the tightly wound minimalist at the center of “The Girl Before,” a psychological thriller by JP Delaney that debuts at No.\u202f5 on the hardcover fiction list. Monkford rents his showpiece house only to alluring young women (who don’t always survive), and he rivals Marie Kondo in his aversion to clutter: The review in the daily New York Times was headlined, ominously, “He Doesn’t Like It When You Leave Your Shampoo Out.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/05delaney/05delaney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/05delaney/05delaney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/05delaney/05delaney-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/05delaney/05delaney-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/05delaney/05delaney-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 592}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/05delaney/05delaney-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 811, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/05delaney/05delaney-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 811, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind the Best Sellers: ‘Girl Before’ Author JP Delaney on Pseudonyms and the Limits of Marie Kondo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Delaney, J P (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Girl Before (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-03T12:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_