, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/885a1e13-ff56-5971-b632-042d36fce1a0', 'word_count': 1047, 'uri': 'nyt://article/885a1e13-ff56-5971-b632-042d36fce1a0'}, {'abstract': 'An Icelandic version of “Dracula” turns out to be a radically different version of the story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/03/books/review/new-life-for-dracula.html', 'snippet': 'An Icelandic version of “Dracula” turns out to be a radically different version of the story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1898, Bram Stoker’s “Dracula” was translated for the first time, into Hungarian. Just a couple of years later, it was published in Icelandic. But it turns out the Icelandic rendering was much more than a translation; it was a radically different version of the story. In a foreword to “Powers of Darkness,” an annotated English-language edition of that version, Dacre Stoker, the great-grandnephew of Bram, says the author “could not have been immune to the influences of the Icelandophiles who surrounded him,” people who had traveled to the country and studied its myths and literature.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/0205-BKS-OpenBook/0205-BKS-OpenBook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/0205-BKS-OpenBook/0205-BKS-OpenBook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books/review/0205-BKS-OpenBook/0205-BKS-OpenBook-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/05/books