e': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-03T21:06:46+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/0b0b4eec-60dd-5a6d-83ad-36e29bb92aa4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/0b0b4eec-60dd-5a6d-83ad-36e29bb92aa4'}, {'abstract': 'The latest in a horror franchise that began in Japan, this film fills in the back story of Samara, the girl who kills via VCR.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/03/movies/rings-review.html', 'snippet': 'The latest in a horror franchise that began in Japan, this film fills in the back story of Samara, the girl who kills via VCR.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Rings,” the latest sequel in the franchise that began with the Japanese film “Ringu,” is short on outright frights, but some effort certainly went into the storytelling. For one thing, by the time this one’s over, fans will know a lot more about the mysterious Samara, the dead girl who continues to terrify the living via a grainy videotape. And those nearing or in college — the demographic being aimed at here — will learn an important lesson: Beware of any professor who tries to recruit you for a research study.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04RINGS/04RINGS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04RINGS/04RINGS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04RINGS/04RINGS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04RINGS/04RINGS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04RINGS/04RINGS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04RINGS/04RINGS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04RINGS/04RINGS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: In ‘Rings,’ Best Not Go to the Videotape', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let’s Not Go to the Tape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Rings (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gutierrez, F Javier (1973- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-03T21:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c406dde-aec2-5b2c-a826-9fa963638dcd', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c406dde-aec2-5b2c-a826-9fa963638dcd'}, {'abstract': 'Under Mary Zimmerman’s direction, this haunting tale of a water nymph’s doomed love for a prince simmers with haunting energy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/03/arts/review-metropolitan-opera-rusalka.html', 'snippet': 'Under Mary Zimmerman’s direction, this haunting tale of a water nymph’s doomed love for a prince simmers with haunting energy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Metropolitan Opera’s new production of Dvorak’s “Rusalka” may be the unlikeliest hit of the season, a staging that takes opera’s answer to “The Little Mermaid” fairy tale and turns it into an almost shockingly dark, sexy drama. The mysterious look of the production, fantastical and ominous, combines with the sensual singing of a handsome cast to create a romantic energy rare at the Met — or at any opera house.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 652}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 943, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB/04-RUSALKA-01-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1885, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Met Opera’s ‘Rusalka’ Is a Dark, Sexy Hit', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Nymph’s Torrid Love', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Rusalka (Opera)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Opolais, Kristine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zimmerman, Mary', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jovanovich, Brandon', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-03T21:11:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/77eda27f-bf08-53c3-adfd-417302430014', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77eda27f-bf08-53c3-adfd-417302430014'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Ashton wrote sweeping histories and intimate studies devoted to individual artists, especially those in the world of Abstract Expressionism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/03/arts/design/dore-ashton-art-critic-who-embraced-and-inhabited-modernism-dies-at-88.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Ashton wrote sweeping histories and intimate studies devoted to individual artists, especially those in the world of Abstract Expressionism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dore Ashton, an art historian and critic who wrote some of the earliest and most insightful histories of Abstract Expressionism and the leading painters of the New York School, died on Monday in the Bronx. She was 88.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04ashton-obit/04ashton-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04ashton-obit/04ashton-obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 771}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04ashton-obit/04ashton-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04ashton-obit/04ashton-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1594, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04ashton-obit/04ashton-obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04ashton-obit/04ashton-obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/04/arts/04ashton-obit/04ashton-obit-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dore Ashton, Art Critic Who Embraced and Inhabited 