, 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javier Corrales', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corrales', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/540367bd-8189-5d5d-9f08-bf08ad0c10b9', 'word_count': 974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/540367bd-8189-5d5d-9f08-bf08ad0c10b9'}, {'abstract': 'Americans who imagine escaping to a liberal utopia are in for a rude awakening.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/05/opinion/meanwhile-in-canada-things-are-just-as-bad.html', 'snippet': 'Americans who imagine escaping to a liberal utopia are in for a rude awakening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TORONTO — When Justin Trudeau was elected prime minister of Canada — with a majority Liberal government, no less — it marked the hopeful end of nearly a decade of Conservative rule. “Sunny ways,” Mr. Trudeau said in his acceptance speech. “This is what positive politics can do.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/opinion/06koul-inyt/06koul-inyt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/06/opinion/06koul-inyt/06koul-inyt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/opinion/06koul-inyt/06koul-inyt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/opinion/06koul-inyt/06koul-inyt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/06