ne, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Six Encounters With Lincoln: A President Confronts Democracy and Its Demons (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pryor, Elizabeth Brown (1951-2015)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-06T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alice Kessler-Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kessler-Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/316bf26d-4203-5904-907e-dc12dec7f478', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/316bf26d-4203-5904-907e-dc12dec7f478'}, {'abstract': 'In a hyperpartisan news environment, spilled secrets can destabilize those in power — and those in the press.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/magazine/the-medias-risky-love-affair-with-leaks.html', 'snippet': 'In a hyperpartisan news environment, spilled secrets can destabilize those in power — and those in the press.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January, BuzzFeed News published a dossier full of sensational and unverified claims compiled by a former British intelligence agent,which described extensive connections between President Trump and the Russian government. Trump responded, as expected, with a tweet. He placed one particular word in suggestive, dismissive quotes: “Intelligence agencies should never have allowed this fake news to ‘leak’ into the public.” Setting aside the dossier’s contents, Trump understood that its status in the public eye depended in part on what it was called. Seen as a piece of opposition research, or a political prop, or a plant, the dossier loses some power. Seen as a leak, however, the dossier seems more likely to be true.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/magazine/12firstwords1/12firstwords1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/12/magazine/12firstwords1/12firstwords1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/magazine/12firstwords1/12firstwords1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/magazine/12firstwords1/12firstwords1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/12/magazine/12firstwords1/12firstwords1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/magazine/12firstwords1/12firstwords1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/magazine/12firstwords1/12firstwords1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1772, 'width': 1772, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Media’s Risky Love Affair With Leaks', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Full Disclosure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classified Information and State Secrets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare and Defense', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BuzzFeed Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-06T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Herrman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herrman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dde8d78e-48df-5908-86ff-3184e9b6c689', 'word_count': 1460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dde8d78e-48df-5908-86ff-3184e9b6c689'}, {'abstract': 'There is a growing tension between the role Germany feels comfortable with, and the one it feels it must play on the world stage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/world/europe/germany-prepares-for-turbulence-in-the-trump-era.html', 'snippet': 'There is a growing tension between the role Germany feels comfortable with, and the one it feels it must play on the world stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — As allies across Europe and Asia adjust to changes brought by President Trump, Germany is in a uniquely difficult position.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/07/world/07int-germany/07int-germany-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/07/world/07int-germany/07int-germany-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'th