 'By Perri Klass, M.d', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Perri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klass', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5f56b05-4cc5-5e82-bddb-4f48a1419270', 'word_count': 996, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5f56b05-4cc5-5e82-bddb-4f48a1419270'}, {'abstract': 'People use the word dizziness when referring to lightheadedness, unsteadiness, motion intolerance, imbalance, floating or a tilting sensation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/well/getting-specific-about-dizziness.html', 'snippet': 'People use the word dizziness when referring to lightheadedness, unsteadiness, motion intolerance, imbalance, floating or a tilting sensation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dizziness is not a disease but rather a symptom that can result from a huge variety of underlying disorders or, in some cases, no disorder at all. Readily determining its cause and how best to treat it — or whether to let it resolve on its own — can depend on how well patients are able to describe exactly how they feel during a dizziness episode and the circumstances under which it usually occurs.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/07/science/07BRODY/07BRODY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/07/science/07BRODY/07BRODY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/07/science/07BRODY/07BRODY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name':