9c-a496-fea7a9b4f35b', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4efc85ae-bc33-579c-a496-fea7a9b4f35b'}, {'abstract': 'Italienne’s weekday pastry counter is a French treat with pains au chocolat, apricot fougasses and burnished almond croissants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/dining/italienne-french-pastry-flatiron-district.html', 'snippet': 'Italienne’s weekday pastry counter is a French treat with pains au chocolat, apricot fougasses and burnished almond croissants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The restaurant Italienne’s mission may be to offer food that’s a blend of French and Northern Italian cuisines, but its new morning pastry counter has a decidedly Gallic accent. Well-burnished, flaky plain croissants, almond ones sheltering marzipan, handsome pains au chocolat, lacy apricot fougasses from Provence and twisted, sugared footlong sacristains are some of the options to eat in or take out. Try an early lunch with the quiche-like egg, cheese and vegetable moulinet: 8 to 11 a.m. weekdays, Italienne, 19 West 24th Street, 212-600-5139, italiennenyc.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08burner-pastry/08burner-pastry-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08burner-pastry/08burner-pastry-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08burner-pastry/08burner-pastry-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08burner-pastry/08burner-pastry-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08burner-pastry/08burner-pastry-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08burner-pastry/08burner-pastry-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08burner-pastry/08burner-pastry-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'French Pastries in the Flatiron District for the Morning Commute', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Breakfast: French Pastries, a Treat for Flatiron Mornings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Italienne (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Croissants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Flatiron District (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-06T15:32:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85352795-cddb-5e55-889a-9f9eae80741d', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85352795-cddb-5e55-889a-9f9eae80741d'}, {'abstract': 'WhistlePig’s Boss Hog rye is aged on a former dairy farm in Shoreham, Vt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/dining/whiskey-rye-whistlepig.html', 'snippet': 'WhistlePig’s Boss Hog rye is aged on a former dairy farm in Shoreham, Vt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The challenge when it comes to American rye whiskeys is to find those that are well aged, as Eric Asimov explained when we tasted young ryes recently. Unexpected demand has left distillers without many bottles aged more than four or five years, leading prices to surge well beyond $1,000 a bottle for older batches. In Shoreham, Vt., at the dairy farm-turned-distillery WhistlePig, the whiskeys are straight rye. Its Boss Hog rye is a forceful, 14-year-old spirit in a handsome bottle with a pewter stopper. It clocks in at about 120 proof (its barrel strength and proof vary slightly) with aromas of toffee and toasted hazelnuts, and hints of burnt orange and honey on the palate. It can take tempering with ice or some spring water: WhistlePig, the Boss Hog Rye Whiskey, $299.99 from drinkupny.com; other WhistlePig whiskeys $71.99 and $109.99; information at whistlepigwhiskey.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08BURNER-WHISKEY/08BURNER-WHISKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08BURNER-WHISKEY/08BURNER-WHISKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08BURNER-WHISKEY/08BURNER-WHISKEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/dining/08BURNER-WHISKEY/08BURNER-WHISKEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'su