rs-slide-O7QU-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU/06tmag-flowers-slide-O7QU-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Studio With Kate Cadbury', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-02-06T19:59:37+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Art', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/96c08042-b18b-5189-be1b-21cdc2c38179', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/96c08042-b18b-5189-be1b-21cdc2c38179'}, {'abstract': 'How the artist Kate Cadbury applies dried, homegrown petals to found photographs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/t-magazine/design/kate-cadbury-flower-pressing-precis.html', 'snippet': 'How the artist Kate Cadbury applies dried, homegrown petals to found photographs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At times, the ground inside the pressed flower artist Kate Cadbury’s potting shed is so strewn with cuttings it resembles a florist’s shop floor. Home to the typical planting paraphernalia (and her husband’s guitars), the space doubles as a studio, where she melds the botanical spoils of her own East London garden with found photographs. Even in midwinter there are beautiful blooms — recently, giant pansies — destined to be pressed. “It’s about preserving a moment,” Cadbury says of the somewhat lost art of dried flowers, as she loads her homespun press with the cheery pansy crop, before covering them with sheets of cardstock and blotting paper to absorb any moisture (water spoils the petals). “Pressing flowers is like going into the dark room,” she explains. “You never quite know what’s going to happen. There’s nothing I would want to do to control the natural process. It’s magical.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG/06tmag-flowers-slide-4SOG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Valentine’s Day Inspiration: Pressed Flowers, on Paper', 'kicker': 'Making It', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Flower Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Valentine's Day", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-06T19:59:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Design & Interiors', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aimee Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aimee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe4ac3aa-7e51-5406-8401-78e1ed5dc26a', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe4ac3aa-7e51-5406-8401-78e1ed5dc26a'}, {'abstract': 'The new L.A. restaurant Destroyer is redefining casual restaurant fare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/t-magazine/food/destroyer-restaurant-los-angeles-jordan-kahn.html', 'snippet': 'The new L.A. restaurant Destroyer is redefining casual restaurant fare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don’t believe Jordan Kahn when he describes his new L.A. restaurant, Destroyer, as “just a breakfast and lunch, community-centered cafe.” The Hayden Tract place — named for the comet that is thought to have ended the earth’s Cretaceous period — is disrupting a golden rule, offering ambitious food that wouldn’t look out of place at the French Laundry (where Kahn trained as a pastry chef) at prices that top out at $15. The approach might be humble — counter service and biodegradable plastic utensils — but the reviews have been grand, with Los Angeles Times critic Jonathan Gold proclaiming the food “the best plant-centered cooking in Los Angeles right now.” Influenced by Copenhagen’s beloved Atelier September and the Japanese attitude that there is no such thing as casual dining, Kahn and his staff apply the same seriousness to toast and jam as they do to a dish of sunchoke, walnut and egg yolk. “It’s hard for me to make a sloppy sandwich,” Kahn says.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '106', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/07/t-magazine/food/destroyer-restaurant/destroyer-restaurant-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/07/t-magazine/food/destroyer-restaurant/destroyer-restaurant-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/07/t-magazine/food/destroyer-restaurant/destroyer-restaurant-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/07/t-mag