2-06T22:21:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Tannenbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tannenbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afd607ad-0bf9-5ba6-b2d2-dd096e4fb168', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afd607ad-0bf9-5ba6-b2d2-dd096e4fb168'}, {'abstract': 'One artist is 3D-printing a sculpture throughout the course of a 7-week show; another is asking visitors to pick apart a copy of a Rodin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/06/t-magazine/art/urs-fischer-anya-gallaccio-sculpture.html', 'snippet': 'One artist is 3D-printing a sculpture throughout the course of a 7-week show; another is asking visitors to pick apart a copy of a Rodin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This month in London, two gallery exhibitions are offering very different experiences each time you return: One artwork is being left to build itself, while another invites its own destruction. The latter, Urs Fischer’s “The Kiss,” is an outsized white Plasticine model of Rodin’s masterpiece, which Fischer has asked visitors to pick apart and reshape over the course of its month-long exhibition at Sadie Coles HQ. Meanwhile, for her show “Beautiful Minds,” showing at neighboring Thomas Dane Gallery, the artist Anya Gallaccio has built a custom 3D ceramic printer and programmed it to construct a clay model of Devils Tower, Wyo. The end result will look like a giant extruded coil pot.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazine/06tmag-art01/06tmag-art01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/06/t-magazin