w', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Boehler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boehler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/16ec0ea7-fbc3-57f9-86c6-e1e0aa398bc4', 'word_count': 1180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16ec0ea7-fbc3-57f9-86c6-e1e0aa398bc4'}, {'abstract': 'On Laura Ingalls Wilder’s 150th birthday, the vision of life depicted in her popular novels still speaks to our country’s promise — and to its divides.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/07/books/review/little-house-laura-ingalls-wilder-anniversary.html', 'snippet': 'On Laura Ingalls Wilder’s 150th birthday, the vision of life depicted in her popular novels still speaks to our country’s promise — and to its divides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing about Laura Ingalls’s birth to a modest Wisconsin family on Feb. 7, 1867, suggested she would become one of the most significant voices in the canon of the American frontier. A century and a half later, the contribution Laura Ingalls Wilder made still seems astonishing — a fact not lost on her publisher. As a new anniversary-themed batch of “Little House on the Prairie” books rolled in this fall — with homespun-looking covers and introductions by luminaries including Laura Bush and Patricia MacLachlan (author of the gentle Newbery Medal-winning novel “Sarah, Plain and Tall”) — I found myself plunging back into the “Little House” world I’d loved as a child, with a strange feeling of urgency.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/books/06wilder/06wilder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/06/books/06wilder/06wilder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/books/06wilder/06wilder-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/books/06wilder/06wilder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 793, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/06/books/06wilder/06wilder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 793}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/books/06wilder/06wilder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 775, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/06/books/06wilder/06wilder-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1550, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding America, Both Red and Blue, in the ‘Little House’ Books', 'kicker': "Reader's Notebook", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A ‘Little House’ Tinged With Red and Blue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilder, Laura Ingalls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Little House on the Prairie (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-07T05:23:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94ea3900-170a-5a0c-84f4-c490be7d860d', 'word_count': 2042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94ea3900-170a-5a0c-84f4-c490be7d860d'}, {'abstract': 'Native Agency, a new collective, is mentoring a dozen photographers from Africa and Latin America to produce overlooked stories in their countries.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/02/07/nurturing-new-storytellers-in-africa-and-latin-america/', 'snippet': 'Native Agency, a new collective, is mentoring a dozen photographers from Africa and Latin America to produce overlooked stories in their countries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For some people, the idea of “serious” photography conjures up dramatic scenes of suffering, violence and poverty. This can be especially so in parts of Latin America and Africa, where careers have been made by foreign journalists who go in looking for drama. While no doubt there are pressing issues in these regions, there are also scenes of daily life, or less dramatic situations, that go unnoticed, slanting how a global audience sees people and places. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nurturing New Storytellers in Africa and Latin America', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mentors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rios, Federico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Native Agency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Press Photo Foundation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colombia', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_dat