: [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/3719c516-45b1-57fc-a3b8-186e509e1e1d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/3719c516-45b1-57fc-a3b8-186e509e1e1d'}, {'abstract': 'You’ll never think of the phrase “fish dive” in the same way again once you have seen one executed in this ballet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/08/arts/dance/sleeping-beauty-new-york-city-ballet.html', 'snippet': 'You’ll never think of the phrase “fish dive” in the same way again once you have seen one executed in this ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks to the composer Tchaikovsky and the choreographer Marius Petipa, the buildup to the heroine’s entrance in “The Sleeping Beauty” is the most potent in all ballet. Act I is Princess Aurora’s 20th birthday. (Many productions change it to her 16th.) The scene begins with the King punishing, then forgiving, some harmless knitting women for using needles at a court that prohibits them. The story has been told already: Aurora will prick her finger and die.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/arts/08beauty-explainer/08beauty-explainer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/08/arts/08beauty-explainer/08beauty-explainer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/08/arts/08beauty-explainer/08beauty-explainer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': 