article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victor Mather', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mather', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ad5d67e-68ae-59a8-8775-6b45282c930b', 'word_count': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ad5d67e-68ae-59a8-8775-6b45282c930b'}, {'abstract': 'The pianist Jonathan Biss explores music written late in life by a range of composers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/08/arts/music/classical-works-from-the-winter-of-life-at-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'The pianist Jonathan Biss explores music written late in life by a range of composers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a series of three programs presented by Carnegie Hall, the thoughtful young American pianist Jonathan Biss explores “The Late Style,” that is, music written late in life by various composers. For the opening one, Mr. Biss is joined by the exciting Brentano String Quartet. Works performed will include selections from Bach’s “The Art of the Fugue” (incomplete at his death), Britten’s Third String Quartet (composed a year before he died) and Beethoven’s Piano Sonata No. 32 (the final one). The modernist Hungarian master Gyorgy Kurtag, still active at 90, is represented by selections from Vol. VII of “Jatekok” (“Games”) for piano. (Wednesday, Feb. 15, Zankel Hall, carnegiehall.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/arts/12GUIDE5/12GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/12/arts/12GUIDE5/12GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0