 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/opinion/sunday/12gray/12gray-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/12/opinion/sunday/12gray/12gray-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/opinion/sunday/12gray/12gray-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/opinion/sunday/12gray/12gray-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1075, 'width': 1074, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Do Social Science Without Data', 'kicker': 'Gray Matter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Social Science Without Data', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bauman, Zygmunt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-09T15:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d81edfa-f659-511d-97f1-7d09aabe3935', 'word_count': 1020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d81edfa-f659-511d-97f1-7d09aabe3935'}, {'abstract': 'In ardently preserving one of the neighborhood’s original lofts, a couple has created a living memory of a vanished era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/09/t-magazine/design/ronnie-sassoon-james-crump-soho-new-york-loft.html', 'snippet': 'In ardently preserving one of the neighborhood’s original lofts, a couple has created a living memory of a vanished era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back when SoHo was a frontier, a place promising infinite possibility for limited funds, the neighborhood’s 19th-century cast-iron buildings began to fill up with struggling artists. The lofts, as they would come to be called — entire floors in former factories and warehouses gone to seed — were drafty in winter, noisy in summer and challenged when it came to plumbing, but who cared when you were getting thousands of feet of raw space for as little as $100 a month? By 1970, galleries were opening, and restaurants and bars soon followed. The artists Gordon Matta-Clark and Carol Goodden opened the restaurant Food on the corner of Prince and Wooster in 1971, fueling the neighborhood’s growing mythology of the artist as urban pioneer. Things got fancier in 1977, when Giorgio DeLuca teamed up with Joel Dean and Jack Ceglic to open the first Dean & DeLuca half a block away on Prince Street. A cavernous space, its white walls and industrial shelving were designed by Ceglic, a painter, to honor the building’s factory roots. The shelves, stocked with then-rare American and European foods (fine olive oils, balsamic vinegars, baby vegetables) became proxy larders for, among others, Donald Judd, who had bought an entire building on Spring Street.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '240', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/09/t-magazine/crump-slide-WLWD/crump-slide-WLWD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 483, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/09/t-magazine/crump-slide-WLWD/crump-slide-WLWD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 483}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/09/t-magazine/crump-slide-WLWD/crump-slide-WLWD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 825, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/09/t-magazine/crump-slide-WLWD/crump-slide-WLWD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1649, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/09/t-magazine/crump-slide-WLWD/crump-slide-WLWD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/09/t-magazine/crump-slide-WLWD/crump-slide-WLWD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/09/t-magazine/crump-slide-WLWD/crump-slide-WLWD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Look of ’70s SoHo — Captured in One Apartment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Look of ’70s SoHo — Captured in One Apartment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crump, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SoHo (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-09T15:08:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazi