, 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-OpenBook/0212-BKS-OpenBook-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 901, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Left Bank', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Left Bank', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paris Review', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Finks: How the CIA Tricked the World's Best Writers (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitney, Joel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-10T17:03:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f30165a1-5e76-55be-9aa4-164591bbc9f6', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f30165a1-5e76-55be-9aa4-164591bbc9f6'}, {'abstract': 'It wasn’t the hope of immortality that goaded me to write: It was obsession.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/10/books/review/headed-for-the-graveyard-of-books.html', 'snippet': 'It wasn’t the hope of immortality that goaded me to write: It was obsession.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s a fine late-summer weekend just before Labor Day, and we’ve come to the Inn at Shelburne Farms, a charming old estate on Lake Champlain, far from the book-glutted world of Manhattan. But not that far: Thanks to my “stay connected whether you want to or not” iPhone, I get updates at all hours of the day and night from Bookish, Goodreads and Lit Hub bannering the new Hot Titles. I can be out on a lake paddling my canoe, and up will pop the shortlist for the Man Booker Prize. If you’re lounging in a rain-rotted Adirondack chair reading “Mrs. Dalloway” on your Kindle, put it aside. The new Jodi Picoult is here.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 203, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 203}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/12/books/review/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote/0212-BKS-AuthorsNote-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': '