': {'original': 'By ALEXANDRA EATON, MEKADO MURPHY, JONAH M. KESSEL and WILL LLOYD', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'EATON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MURPHY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Jonah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/1b442d6e-2453-5f3c-abef-e306d69788c8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/1b442d6e-2453-5f3c-abef-e306d69788c8'}, {'abstract': 'Nordur, a coarse Icelandic sea salt, makes its way to America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/13/dining/sea-salt-iceland-nordur.html', 'snippet': 'Nordur, a coarse Icelandic sea salt, makes its way to America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alda the mermaid, the symbol of this Nordur Salt and guardian of the supply, sounds straight out of the Metropolitan Opera’s new production of Dvorak’s “Rusalka,” about a water nymph. The coarse, flaky salt from Iceland is made by evaporating seawater. While much of the geothermal manufacturing process has been updated since 1753, the salt is still raked by hand. Use it as a finishing salt, dusted on potatoes, a salad or poached eggs just before serving: Nordur Arctic Sea Salt Flakes, $11.99 for 8.8 ounces, salttraders.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/15/dining/15BURNER-SALT/15BURNER-SALT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/15/dining/15BURNER-SALT/15BURNER-SALT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight