y-slide-4R83-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1528, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Hair Products for a Lived-in Look', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2017-02-14T19:36:23+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Entertainment', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/d66296ce-d8bc-53c9-8383-15bdf163aa69', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d66296ce-d8bc-53c9-8383-15bdf163aa69'}, {'abstract': 'Hero or villain, black or white, gay or straight — most female characters seem to have the same perfect locks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/14/t-magazine/fashion/bad-television-hair-beauty.html', 'snippet': 'Hero or villain, black or white, gay or straight — most female characters seem to have the same perfect locks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I judge television shows by the women’s hair. It turns out this is a binary judgment: Either the women have TV hair, or they don’t. What is TV hair? It’s shiny, long, has obviously been styled with a curling iron at the ends, and looks like that of a beauty pageant contestant. (The style is known as “The Cosmo” in the parlance of Drybar, the blowout salon that has more than 70 locations across America.)', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '120', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/t-magazine/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/t-magazine/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/t-magazine/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/t-magazine/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/t-magazine/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/t-magazine/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/t-magazine/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0/19tmag-01look_tvhair-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1187, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On TV, Does Bad Hair Signal a Better Show?', 'kicker': 'On Beauty', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bad Hair', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hair', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-14T19:36:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Fashion & Beauty', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Witt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Witt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4602a633-0d4a-5f97-bf48-c30d57312a51', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4602a633-0d4a-5f97-bf48-c30d57312a51'}, {'abstract': 'Mo Willems and Oliver Jeffers talk about turning their children’s books into plays.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/14/books/review/mo-willems-oliver-jeffers-childrens-books-plays.html', 'snippet': 'Mo Willems and Oliver Jeffers talk about turning their children’s books into plays.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mo Willems and Oliver Jeffers — two of the most beloved, and singular, creators of children’s picture books working today — have both seen their literary creations head to the stage. The musical “Elephant & Piggie’s We Are in a Play!,” based on Willems’s Elephant & Piggie series, recently finished a run at the New Victory Theater in New York and is setting out on a national tour; “The Way Back Home,” a puppet production based on Jeffers’s book of the same title, will be at the New Victory from March 10 through March 26. The two authors talked to Maria Russo, The Times’s children’s books editor, about the thrills and embarrassments of children’s theater and their books’ very different journeys to the stage. These are edited excerpts from the conversation.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/15/arts/00willems/00willems-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 624, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/15/arts/00willems/00willems-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 624}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/15/arts/00willems/00willems-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024