mbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Afropunk Remixes the Songbook of Black Protest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop; Remixing Songs of Black Protest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APOLLO THEATER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glasper, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-15T14:51:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/125aa8c9-edd2-5c2f-adfa-2e5a42ab8cc9', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/125aa8c9-edd2-5c2f-adfa-2e5a42ab8cc9'}, {'abstract': 'The orchestra, led by Franz Welser-Möst, opens a three-part series at Carnegie Hall on Feb. 24', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/arts/music/the-vienna-philharmonic-remembers-schubert.html', 'snippet': 'The orchestra, led by Franz Welser-Möst, opens a three-part series at Carnegie Hall on Feb. 24', 'lead_paragraph': 'Franz Schubert’s death at 31 cut short the life of one of the great talents of the 19th century. Few experiences bring home that fact as acutely as performances of his Symphony No. 8, nicknamed “Unfinished,” which forms the centerpiece of a three-part concert series by the Vienna Philharmonic Orchestra that opens on Friday, Feb. 24. Led by the conductor Franz Welser-Möst, this stellar ensemble also presents Schubert’s Overture to “Die Zauberharfe” and his Ninth Symphony alongside Strauss’s popular tone poem “Ein Heldenleben” and Schoenberg’s luscious “Verklärte Nacht.” The concert on Friday also features the American premiere of “Time Recycling” by René Staar, a longtime violinist with this orchestra. (carnegiehall.org)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE7/19GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE7/19GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE7/19GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE7/19GUIDE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1150, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE7/19GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE7/19GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE7/19GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Vienna Philharmonic Remembers Schubert', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Classical; A Vienna Tribute  to Schubert', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vienna Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schubert, Franz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Welser-Most, Franz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-15T14:52:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dc9286e-c181-5cd9-841f-fc1316fa5daa', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dc9286e-c181-5cd9-841f-fc1316fa5daa'}, {'abstract': 'During the visit of the royal family in Eastbourne, the Salvation Army refused an appeal by the authorities to suspend their outdoor processions.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/1892-the-blood-and-fire-army-resisted-by-police/', 'snippet': 'During the visit of the royal family in Eastbourne, the Salvation Army refused an appeal by the authorities to suspend their outdoor processions.', 'lead_paragraph': ' LONDON — Scenes of great turbulence were caused by the operations of the Salvation Army at Eastbourne yesterday. The Salva\xadtionists refused an appeal by the authorities to suspend their outdoor processions over the period occupied by the visit of the Prince and Princess of Wales and family, and this circumstance, together with the fact that a poll of the borough is now being taken on the Sunday Bands question, appeared to incense the crowd, who attacked the Salvationist procession at several points of their route.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1892: The “Blood and Fire” Army Resisted by Police', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royal Families', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Salvation Army', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-15T14:55:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcdc04b1-9b4a-53fb-a421-f2bde1272ac0', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcdc04b1-9b4a-53fb-a421-f2bde1272ac0'}, {'abstract': 'The company will also focus on the music of Richard Rodgers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/arts/dance/new-york-city-ballet-pays-homage-to-jerome-robbins.html', 'snippet': 'The company will also focus on the music of Richard Rodgers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Broadway likes to claim Jerome Robbins as one of its own, but New York City Ballet knows where he found his choreographic chops: in the land of tendus. For the final week of the winter season, the company pays homage to this choreographer with a program that shows off his magnificent range: “Glass Pieces” is a sweeping, energetic work to Philip Glass; its opposite, “Moves,” is austere, scrupulous and performed in silence; and Chopin provides music for “The Concert,” a comic masterpiece in which the dreams and fantasies of the audience take center stage.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE4/19GUIDE4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thum