': 'nyt://article/edd6a5eb-ea77-5015-976a-0dc9e51d6e97'}, {'abstract': 'Jews and Catholics were ranked the highest, but even atheists, near the bottom, were viewed more positively than three years ago, despite a divisive election.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/us/poll-religion.html', 'snippet': 'Jews and Catholics were ranked the highest, but even atheists, near the bottom, were viewed more positively than three years ago, despite a divisive election.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After an election year that stirred up animosity across racial and religious lines, a new survey has found that Americans are actually feeling warmer toward people in nearly every religious group — including Muslims — than they did three years ago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Americans Rate Religious Groups, and Most Gain, Including Muslims', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Research Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim Americans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Atheism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-15T15:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurie Goodstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53343ac6-b8de-5af3-8c8f-8e8a77a3aab8', 'word_count': 562, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53343ac6-b8de-5af3-8c8f-8e8a77a3aab8'}, {'abstract': 'The exhibit “To Organize Delirium,” at the Art Institute of Chicago, features more than 200 works from this trailblazing Brazilian artist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/arts/design/celebrating-helio-oiticicas-prodigious-run.html', 'snippet': 'The exhibit “To Organize Delirium,” at the Art Institute of Chicago, features more than 200 works from this trailblazing Brazilian artist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The brilliant Brazilian artist Hélio Oiticica was a prodigy and a trailblazer. When he died in 1980 at 42, his fast-moving career had contributed to several waves of postwar innovation. He joined Rio de Janeiro’s avant-garde circles while still a teenager, making crisp geometric paintings inspired by Mondrian’s abstractions. Next, with sculptures both suspended and earthbound, he took color into three dimensions.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE5/19GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE5/19GUIDE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE5/19GUIDE5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE5/19GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 607, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE5/19GUIDE5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 607}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE5/19GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1013, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE5/19GUIDE5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2044, 'width': 2022, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Celebrating Hélio Oiticica’s Prodigious Run', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Celebrating  a Prodigious Run', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ART INSTITUTE OF CHICAGO', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oiticica, Helio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-15T15:04:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c6e5b43-a6db-5f86-84e3-e2f21d6aaa22', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c6e5b43-a6db-5f86-84e3-e2f21d6aaa22'}, {'abstract': 'This new CMT series portrays the formative years of some future superstars and the label that changed American music.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/arts/television/sun-records-goes-once-upon-a-time-with-elvis-and-cash.html', 'snippet': 'This new CMT series portrays the formative years of some future superstars and the label that changed American music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The genesis of some titanic musical careers is the focus of “Sun Records,” an eight-part drama that begins Thursday, Feb. 23, on CMT (right after “Nashville”). You know the names — Elvis Presley (Drake Milligan), Johnny Cash (Kevin Fonteyne), Jerry Lee Lewis (Christian Lees), Carl Perkins (Dustin Ingram). And perhaps you already know the gist of their intertwining stories from having seen “Million Dollar Quartet,” the stage musical.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE1/19GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE1/19GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE1/19GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE1/19GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 495, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE1/19GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 495}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE1/19GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 845, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE1/19GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1689, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sun Records’ Goes Once Upon a Time With Elvis and Cash', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television; Elvis and Cash, Once Upon a Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Country Music Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Sun Records (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-15T15:05:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02719f9b-1849-573b-aa6b-fad2e3e862c5', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02719f9b-1849-573b-aa6b-fad2e3e862c5'}, {'abstract': 'Branden Jacobs-Jenkins’s new play is part of the Signature Theater Company’s Residency Five program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/theater/in-everybody-a-new-angle-on-race.html', 'snippet': 'Branden Jacobs-Jenkins’s new play is part of the Signature Theater Company’s Residency Five program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The continually surprising playwright Branden Jacobs-Jenkins likes to rattle cages, especially those claustrophobic, categorical prisons in which we like to confine ourselves — and one another. He has explored the American racial divide through a dizzying range of variations on classic, creaky forms: minstrel show (“Neighbors”); 19th-century melodrama (“An Octoroon”); and even the Southern gothic potboiler, replete with closeted skeletons (“Appropriate”). Now he has moved on to the most cosmic of theatrical genres with a contemporary take on the 15th-century “Everyman” morality plays, which he has given the more embracing title of “Everybody.” As part of the Signature Theater Company’s Residency Five program, this production — now in previews — has been staged by the fast-rising director Lila Neugebauer and features a cast of Off Broadway stalwarts, whose specific parts will be determined anew at each performance nightly — by lottery, if you please. (Through March 12, signaturetheatre.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE2/19GUIDE2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE2/19GUIDE2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE2/19GUIDE2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE2/19GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE2/19GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE2/19GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/arts/19GUIDE2/19GUIDE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In ‘Everybody,’ a New Angle on Race', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Theater; A New Angle  on an Old Line', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Signature Theater Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jacobs-Jenkins, Branden', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neugebauer, Lila', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Everybody (Play)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-15T15:07:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8d53aa1-a954-50e1-a686-d3ecc664f41a', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8d53aa1-a954-50e1-a686-d3ecc664f41a'}, {'abstract': 'Two start-ups are competing in the unusual business of selling catnip-laced “wines” to cat owners. So far, the drinks’ biggest fans are humans, not cats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/15/business/smallbusiness/wine-products-for-cats-and-dogs.html', 'snippet': 'Two start-ups are competing in the unusual business of selling catnip-laced “wines” to cat owners. So far, the drinks’ biggest fans are humans, not cats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OAKLAND, Calif. — Why drink alone when you can drink with your pet?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/16/business/16EBIZ4/16EBIZ4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/16/business/16EBIZ4/16EBIZ4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/16/business/16EBIZ4/16EBIZ4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {