ernative and Renewable Energy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-16T20:11:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Lubell and Burton Richter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Lubell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Burton', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd010ef3-fd99-5b86-843e-39ef4bf87226', 'word_count': 849, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd010ef3-fd99-5b86-843e-39ef4bf87226'}, {'abstract': 'Although it claims to explore contemporary art in Cuba from the 1970s on, this show isn’t a representative survey. Wild and noisy are exactly what it is not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/16/arts/design/a-bumpy-journey-from-havana-to-new-york-ends-at-wild-noise-ruido-salvaje.html', 'snippet': 'Although it claims to explore contemporary art in Cuba from the 1970s on, this show isn’t a representative survey. Wild and noisy are exactly what it is not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Storm clouds have piled up around “Wild Noise/Ruido Salvaje,” an exhibition of contemporary Cuban work at the Bronx Museum of the Arts. The show was planned a few years ago as a two-part mutual exchange of permanent collection work between the Bronx Museum and the National Museum of Fine Arts in Havana. In May 2015, the Bronx material, a juicy globalist grab bag, made the trip to Cuba without a hitch and returned home safely. The prearranged shipment of Cuban art from the National Museum to New York, however, hit a roadblock when, according to the Bronx Museum, Cuban authorities refused to let any of it leave the island.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 