nts-family-phone-bill-sleepovers-sudoku-strangers.html', 'snippet': 'Also, a 13-year-old sleepover addict, Sudoku strangers on a train and a parental misunderstanding.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My husband and I (a recent second marriage for both of us) have six grown children between us, ages 19 to 29. We are still paying all of their cellphone bills, including the recently married eldest’s. How do you recommend that we get gainfully employed young adults to start paying their own bills? I know that family mobile plans are often cheaper than individual plans, but shouldn’t the kids offer to reimburse us? My husband is not worried about this, but I am.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Family Planning ... for Your Phones', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kids’ Phones, Parents’ Dimes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-16T22:39:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42ddac84-85b1-5f6e-8653-c0bcffa27c9e', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42ddac84-85b1-5f6e-8653-c0bcffa27c9e'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/16/books/review/10-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lincoln. A fitting subject for Presidents’ Day weekend, and we’ve got two books that feature our 16th president. In fiction: George Saunders’s much anticipated debut novel, “Lincoln in the Bardo.” And in nonfiction: “Six Encounters with Lincoln,” a new perspective on the president by the historian Elizabeth Brown Pryor, who sadly died prior to the book’s publication. Two other timely books were written with young readers in mind — a new biography of Frederick Douglass and another of the artist Jean-Michel Basquiat. It’s books like these that inspire.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/books/review/19edchoice-1/19edchoice-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/books/review/19edchoice-1/19edchoice-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/books/review/19edchoice-1/19edchoice-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/books/review/19edchoice-1/19edchoice-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/books/review/19edchoice-1/19edchoice-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/books/review/19edchoice-1/19edchoice-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/books/review/19edchoice-1/19edchoice-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 1034, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '10 New Books We Recommend This Week', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-16T22:40:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd48a538-40ec-585d-b831-bd2a49533b8c', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd48a538-40ec-585d-b831-bd2a49533b8c'}, {'abstract': 'Anne Hamilton’s film seems full of suspenseful promise as it opens on an isolated swath of the country’s heartland, but the story finds no traction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/16/movies/american-fable-review.html', 'snippet': 'Anne Hamilton’s film seems full of suspenseful promise as it opens on an isolated swath of the country’s heartland, but the story finds no traction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fading amber light, the waving green fields, the meandering young girl — opens on a pretty pastoral scene. Still, as the gliding camera keeps pace with this girl, a strange undercurrent disturbs the air, creating pinpricks of doubt about just how tranquil the scene is. There’s something about how red her top looks in the golden light, something about the quiet, something which suggests that the camera isn’t merely on the prowl but also out for blood. It’s a nice opening for a movie that spirals into nonsense.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘American Fable,’ Pastoral With a Wolf Howling', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Pastoral, With a Wolf Howling', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'American Fable (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Anne (Film Director)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Peyton (2004- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-16T22:42:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f15e87ed-961f-59f5-860d-85574adc1243', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f15e87ed-961f-59f5-860d-85574adc1243'}, {'abstract': 'Scores of sideshow paintings portray a bustling society on the cusp of dramatic change in 19th-century France.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/16/arts/design/cirque-du-seurat-at-the-metropolitan-museum-of-art.html', 'snippet': 'Scores of sideshow paintings portray a bustling society on the cusp of dramatic change in 19th-century France.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the great paintings of early modernism, and of Post-Impressionism, was created in Paris beginning in the spring of 1887. Over the next 12 months, the young, ambitious Georges Seurat meticulously distilled his interests in art, stylization, optical science and popular entertainment into the alluring, enigmatic urban nocturne that is “Circus Sideshow (Parade de Cirque).”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/17/arts/SEURAT2/SEURAT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/17/arts/SEURAT2/SEURAT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017