d_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6615254c-2a2f-5da0-acc9-4142088fc85b'}, {'abstract': 'Allaying suspicions on the Ukrainian front, with some unexpected assistance. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/17/magazine/the-cat-named-morphine.html', 'snippet': 'Allaying suspicions on the Ukrainian front, with some unexpected assistance. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several months ago, my editor sent me to the Ukrainian front lines to write an article about the volunteer medics working there. I have covered the Ukrainian conflict since the Maidan uprising in Kiev in 2013. I was in Crimea when the little green men appeared there, and I took photographs during the siege in Debaltseve. Over time, I heard a lot about the organizations that send doctors, medics and medicine to the eastern provinces to tend to the wounded in the war with Russian-backed separatists. I had lobbied to do photo reportage on these brave civilians, so when I finally got permission, I didn’t delay in flying from Budapest to Kiev. My plan was to travel to Kostyantynivka by train and meet the volunteers at the rail station.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19lives/19mag-19lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19lives/19mag-19lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19lives/19mag-19lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19lives/19mag-19lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 844, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19lives/19mag-19lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 844}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19lives/19mag-19lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 729, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19lives/19mag-19lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1457, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cat Named Morphine ', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Cat Named Morphine ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cats', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crimea (Ukraine)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-17T10:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sandor Jaszberenyi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sandor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaszberenyi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a58fb84-4221-5575-a172-5ef0ef47ce8a', 'word_count': 830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a58fb84-4221-5575-a172-5ef0ef47ce8a'}, {'abstract': 'Go to poorly sampled places. Look in museums too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/17/magazine/how-to-discover-a-species.html', 'snippet': 'Go to poorly sampled places. Look in museums too.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Go to a place that is known to be poorly sampled — a place that not enough people have gone to actually collect things or a place that harbors a rich diversity of organisms,” says José C.E. Mendoza, curator of crabs at the Lee Kong Chian Natural History Museum in Singapore. “I go to tropical marine areas, like coral reefs or the deep sea — any place that has water.” Look in nooks; turn over rocks. Mendoza says that for him, “the best time to go is at night, when the crabs are usually more active.” Academic settings offer another kind of environment in which to go hunting. “A lot of new species are found in museum collections — samples that have remained unstudied and unsorted and no one knows what to call them until a person comes in to name them.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19tip/19tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19tip/19tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19tip/19tip-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19tip/19tip-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19tip/19tip-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 668}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/19/magazine/19tip/19tip-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 920, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumb