e rule unless it leads to something they don’t like.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wielding their power to take a cudgel to local laws, Iowa’s Republican-controlled Statehouse is moving to make workers in four counties take a pay cut. Legislators plan to roll back minimum-wage laws that local governments passed to help their working-poor constituents.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/18/education/21tues3-web/21tues3-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/18/education/21tues3-web/21tues3-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/18/education/21tues3-web/21tues3-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/18/education/21tues3-web/21tues3-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/18/education/21tues3-web/21tues3-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/18/education/21tues3-web/21tues3-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/18/education/21tues3-web/21tues3-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Iowa’s G.O.P. Statehouse Shows the Locals Who’s Boss', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'G.O.P. Statehouse Shows the Locals Who’s Boss', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collective Bargaining', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Local Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-21T08:21:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/284e86fa-6ab5-58e5-ab56-9fc233c45fd9', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/284e86fa-6ab5-58e5-ab56-9fc233c45fd9'}, {'abstract': 'Emine Ziyatdinova, a Crimean Tatar, has been documenting the changes in her homeland in the years since the Russian Federation annexed the Crimean peninsula from Ukraine.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/02/21/a-crimean-tatar-documents-her-annexed-homeland/', 'snippet': 'Emine Ziyatdinova, a Crimean Tatar, has been documenting the changes in her homeland in the years since the Russian Federation annexed the Crimean peninsula from Ukraine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Emine Ziyatdinova has been living through the unsettling changes in Crimea since 2014, when Russia annexed the peninsula from Ukraine and a disputed referendum approved it. She is among the minority who opposed it: She is a Crimean Tatar whose grandparents — like others of her ethnic group — had been expelled by Joseph Stalin in the 1944 and exiled to Uzbekistan. Her family returned in 1990 after Soviet authorities allowed Crimean Tatars to return in 1989, but their homecoming has proved to be a nervous, tenuous existence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Crimean Tatar Documents Her Annexed Homeland', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tatars (Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ziyatdinova, Emine', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Crimea (Ukraine)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-21T09:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3999dbb-82fb-507e-8b8b-d92bf9621c6b', 'word_count': 979, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3999dbb-82fb-507e-8b8b-d92bf9621c6b'}, {'abstract': 'The singer and actor on the “La La Land” controversy, forms of resistance and playing the role of Frederick Douglass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/21/magazine/john-legend-cant-pretend-times-are-normal.html', 'snippet': 'The singer and actor on the “La La Land” controversy, forms of resistance and playing the role of Frederick Douglass.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You’ll be playing Frederick Douglass in the second season of WGN America’s “Underground.” Do you think it’s possible that President Trump was thinking of you when he described Douglass as someone who’s done an amazing job? Yeah, I’ve been doing a lot of good things lately, and I’m getting recognized more and more! I don’t think that Trump has read a book in his adult life, or that he knows anything about American history, black history, any history. I don’t think he knows what’s in the Constitution. I don’t think he knows anything about Civil War history, or just about any kind of history. We can’t expect him to know much of anything.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26talk1/26talk1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26talk1/26talk1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26talk1/26talk1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26talk1/26talk1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 1451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26talk1/26talk1-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26talk1/26talk1-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 424, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26talk1/26talk1-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 847, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John Legend Can’t Pretend Times Are Normal', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'John Legend Can’t Pretend Times Are Normal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights Movement (1954-68)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douglass, Frederick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-21T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Ana Marie Cox', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Ana', 'lastname': 'Marie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b285dfd6-5a63-595e-8655-856e9fecb19a', 'word_count': 698, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b285dfd6-5a63-595e-8655-856e9fecb19a'}, {'abstract': 'The arguments over the confirmation of the new secretary of education were about something bigger: which government institutions benefit which citizens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/21/magazine/have-we-lost-sight-of-the-promise-of-public-schools.html', 'snippet': 'The arguments over the confirmation of the new secretary of education were about something bigger: which government institutions benefit which citizens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the days leading up to and after Betsy DeVos’s confirmation as secretary of education, a hashtag spread across Twitter: #publicschoolproud. Parents and teachers tweeted photos of their kids studying, performing, eating lunch together. People of all races tweeted about how public schools changed them, saved them, helped them succeed. The hashtag and storytelling was a rebuttal to DeVos, who called traditional public schools a “dead end” and who bankrolled efforts to pass reforms in Michigan, her home state, that would funnel public funds in the form of vouchers into religious and privately operated schools and encouraged the proliferation of for-profit charter schools. The tweets railed against DeVos’s labeling of public schools as an industry that needed to adopt the free-market principles of competition and choice. #Publicschoolproud was seen as an effort to show that public schools still mattered.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26firstwords/26firstwords-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 561, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26firstwords/26firstwords-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 561}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26firstwords/26firstwords-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 958, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26firstwords/26firstwords-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1428, 'width': 1526, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26firstwords/26firstwords-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26firstwords/26firstwords-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26firstwords/26firstwords-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Have We Lost Sight of the Promise of Public Schools?', 'kicker': 'First Words', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Common Sense', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeVos, Elizabeth (1958- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'English Language', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-21T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikole Hannah-Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hannah-Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b56a9cc5-186a-575b-953d-a06c15a32017', 'word_count': 1525, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b56a9cc5-186a-575b-953d-a06c15a32017'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers tried to pin down why our brains can be susceptible to errors in perception that make us mix up mouth movements and sounds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/21/science/lip-reading-mcgurk-effect.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers tried to pin down why our brains can be susceptible to errors in perception that make us mix up mouth movements and sounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The good news is, the human brain is flexible and efficient. This helps us make sense of the wo