alue': 'Sahib (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-21T17:42:16+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/f1a247ef-cc58-5aa4-bcd3-068f6c7145f3', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/f1a247ef-cc58-5aa4-bcd3-068f6c7145f3'}, {'abstract': 'The menu darts from Kashmir to South India and beyond at the newest restaurant from the prolific Hemant Mathur (with help from Shiva Natarajan).', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/21/dining/sahib-review-indian-restaurant-nyc.html', 'snippet': 'The menu darts from Kashmir to South India and beyond at the newest restaurant from the prolific Hemant Mathur (with help from Shiva Natarajan).', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anybody who tries to tell you that the place to go for Indian food is Jackson Heights, Queens, hasn’t been there in a long time. Beyond question, Jackson Heights is the first stop for anyone seeking an education in the Tibetan syllabus: momos filled with brothy beef under their bellybutton pleats; laphing, noodles neatly rolled and sliced like strudel and set loose in a puddle of chile oil; steaming, salty, bracing cups of yak butter tea. Most of the neighborhood’s places for samosas and naan, though, either are gone or have faded into insignificance.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sahib, in Curry Hill, Lets You Eat All Over India', 'kicker': 'Restaurant Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Culinary Exploration of India, in One Menu', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kips Bay (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mathur, Hemant', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sahib (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-21T17:42:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pete Wells', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pete', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wells', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/870f4ed7-50fc-5a7a-9518-ec287d51e5a3', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/870f4ed7-50fc-5a7a-9518-ec287d51e5a3'}, {'abstract': 'The episode between the police and residents came two days after President Trump made provocative claims about migration and crime in Sweden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/21/world/europe/stockholm-sweden-riots-trump.html', 'snippet': 'The episode between the police and residents came two days after President Trump made provocative claims about migration and crime in Sweden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STOCKHOLM — Residents in a northwestern suburb predominantly inhabited by immigrants have clashed with police officers, two days after President Trump unleashed a vague but pointed critique of Sweden’s migration policies.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Clashes in Stockholm Suburb Draw Attention to Trump’s Remarks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Trump Remark, an Unpleasant Echo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sweden', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Stockholm (Sweden)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-21T18:01:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christina Anderson and Sewell Chan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sewell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0305ff54-1e35-5a9a-b900-e76408ee95dc', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0305ff54-1e35-5a9a-b900-e76408ee95dc'}, {'abstract': 'The Trapizzino combines a focaccialike bread with hearty Italian fillings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/21/dining/trapizzino-nyc-rome-street-snack.html', 'snippet': 'The Trapizzino combines a focaccialike bread with hearty Italian fillings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Trapizzino is a thick triangle of crusty bread split open and filled with classics like hearty meatballs, chicken cacciatore or even braised tongue in green sauce. This Roman-style snack is the work of Stefano Callegari, who has a number of such places in the Eternal City. The bread is the edible container, like a pita pocket. “It’s a way to get people to appreciate the traditional food of Rome,” Mr. Callegari said. At his new Lower East Side location, opening on Wednesday, you get a substantial amount of food for $6. Suppli, which are like oversize arancini but with a creamier filling, are another specialty: Trapizzino, 144 Orchard Street (Rivington Street), 212-475-2555, trapizzinousa.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/dining/22BURNER-BREAD/22BURNER-BREAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/22/dining/22BURNER-BREAD/22BURNER-BREAD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/dining/22BURNER-BREAD/22BURNER-BREAD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/dining/22BURNER-BREAD/22BURNER-BREAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/22/dining/22BURNER-BREAD/22BURNER-BREAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/dining/22BURNER-BREAD/22BURNER-BREAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/dining/22BURNER-BREAD/22BURNER-BREAD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Portable Roman Snack on the Lower East Side', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Stuff: Portable Tastes of Rome  Come to Lower East Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snack Foods', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Trapizzino (Manhattan, NY, 