olitics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-22T09:35:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/021f810b-3be6-51b2-878b-c53d8b047839', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/021f810b-3be6-51b2-878b-c53d8b047839'}, {'abstract': 'Is there a more quintessentially urban act?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/22/magazine/letter-of-recommendation-subway-napping.html', 'snippet': 'Is there a more quintessentially urban act?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In high school, I lived my longest days. I chiseled my morning routine into a trim half-hour (shower, clothes, Cheerios), and at 6:40 a.m., I raced to the subway, squeezed into a car, gazed uncomprehendingly at my chemistry notes, popped out onto Chambers Street and shot westward to school. And when classes concluded at 3:40, I did not, like a normal person, go home; I went to the school-newspaper office and edited, and wrote, and wondered if anyone would ever kiss me, and eventually, after darkness had fallen, drifted back across Chambers to board the train uptown.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26lor/26mag-26lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26lor/26mag-26lor-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/