wing the death of Norma McCorvey, known as “Jane Roe” in the landmark Roe v. Wade ruling that legalized abortion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/opinion/23pubedart/23pubedart-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/22/opinion/23pubedart/23pubedart-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/opinion/23pubedart/23pubedart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/opinion/23pubedart/23pubedart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/opinion/23pubedart/23pubedart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/22/opinion/23pubedart/23pubedart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/22/opinion/23pubedart/23pubedart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case of the Wrong Justice', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCorvey, Norma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roe v Wade (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-22T10:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Public Editor', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Spayd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Spayd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/73f5c9e4-2eee-5eac-8b4b-8bc0c99d5a33', 'word_count': 897, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73f5c9e4-2eee-5eac-8b4b-8bc0c99d5a33'}, {'abstract': 'The Atlantic Coast Conference is within striking distance of the record for N.C.A.A. tournament berths — 11, by the Big East in 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/22/sports/ncaabasketball/acc-basketball-ncaa-tournament.html', 'snippet': 'The Atlantic Coast Conference is within striking distance of the record for N.C.A.A. tournament berths — 11, by the Big East in 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cream of the Atlantic Coast Conference played Wednesday night, and the early game proved worthy of the league’s sterling reputation this year, as Syracuse’s John Gillon banked in a buzzer-beater from deep to give the Orange a 78-75 victory over No. 10 Duke. Coupled with No. 7 Louisville’s matchup against No. 8 North Carolina later on, the night was a showcase for four programs that all have won national titles, feature Hall of Fame coaches and (especially given Syracuse’s victory) will qualify for next month’s N.C.A.A. tournament.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201