ataraman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Najar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b69a2a30-a3b1-5d7b-a963-b1bbd548dc67', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b69a2a30-a3b1-5d7b-a963-b1bbd548dc67'}, {'abstract': 'The album David Longstreth made after the end of his relationship with his former band mate Amber Coffman uses R&B tech but speaks from the heart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/22/arts/music/dirty-projectors-self-titled-review.html', 'snippet': 'The album David Longstreth made after the end of his relationship with his former band mate Amber Coffman uses R&B tech but speaks from the heart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Longstreth, the songwriter and singer who records as Dirty Projectors, gets right to the point as his new album begins. “I don’t know why you abandoned me,” he sings in “Keep Your Name,” “You were my soul and partner.” Yes, “Dirty Projectors” — self-titled, like a new band’s debut — is a breakup album, based on the real-life split of both a romance and a band. Mr. Longstreth and Amber Coffman, who sang and played guitar in Dirty Projectors, parted in the wake of the band’s last tour in 2013. The crisis was both personal and musical — and, it turned out, transformative.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/23/arts/23DIRTYPROJECTORS2/23DIRTYPROJECTORS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/23/arts/23DIRTYPROJECTORS2/23DIRTYPROJECTORS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 