 'Chelsea Clinton', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Chelsea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-23T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/06875bf3-ff41-5cef-80de-9a90fc224c3a', 'word_count': 2306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06875bf3-ff41-5cef-80de-9a90fc224c3a'}, {'abstract': 'Many Americans need jobs, or want better jobs, while employers have good jobs they can’t fill. Matching them up is the tricky part.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/23/magazine/retraining-jobs-unemployment.html', 'snippet': 'Many Americans need jobs, or want better jobs, while employers have good jobs they can’t fill. Matching them up is the tricky part.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Nathan Kecy graduated from Plymouth State University in New Hampshire a decade ago with a bachelor’s degree in communications, he found himself with about $10,000 in debt and few clear career options. He first found work as a door-to-door salesman (“a pyramid scheme,” he recalls) and then in telemarketing. Finally he landed a job as an infrastructure specialist for Datamatic, a Texas-based water-meter-technology company. He was traveling across the country installing meters, making a decent salary. But he lost his job after the company restructured in 2012, he said, and soon he found that his skills weren’t easily transferable to a new field; Datamatic’s technology was proprietary, and his expertise in the company’s installation program wasn’t appealing to employers outside that particular industry. He tried going into business with a friend, but the relationship soured. By then he had a baby and a fiancée, and he felt stuck.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26retraining/26retraining-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26retraining/26retraining-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26retraining/26retraining-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26retraining/26retraining-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26retraining/26retraining-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 681}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26retraining/26retraining-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 903, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26retraining/26retraining-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1806, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Retraining Paradox', 'kicker': 'The Future of Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Retraining', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shortages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-23T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth Graham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/331ad6b0-015d-5c2c-85fc-dec187aa6dbb', 'word_count': 2442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/331ad6b0-015d-5c2c-85fc-dec187aa6dbb'}, {'abstract': 'The most important frontier for robots is not the work they take from humans but the work they do with humans — which requires learning on both sides.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/23/magazine/learning-to-love-our-robot-co-workers.html', 'snippet': 'The most important frontier for robots is not the work they take from humans but the work they do with humans — which requires learning on both sides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The robots were Joe McGillivray’s idea. The first one arrived at Dynamic Group in Ramsey, Minn., by pickup truck in two cardboard boxes. With a mixture of excitement and trepidation, McGillivray watched as a vendor unpacked two silver tubes, assorted blue-and-gray joints and a touch screen and put them all together. When he was finished 10 minutes later, McGillivray beheld an arm that, had its segments not all been able to swivel 360 degrees, might have belonged to a very large N.B.A. player or a fairly small giant. Its “shoulder” was mounted to a waist-high pedestal on wheels. If it were to hail someone across the room, its “elbow” would reach eye level. Below its “wrist,” which was triple-jointed for extra dexterity, there were sockets for various attachments. McGillivray, not sure yet if he wanted to keep the contraption, stuck a piece of clear tape to the wrist and drew a happy face on it, which made the arm look a bit as if it were putting on a puppet show. He hoped that this would help it look nonthreatening.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26machines/26mag-26machines-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26machines/26mag-26machines-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26machines/26mag-26machines-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26machines/26mag-26machines-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26machines/26mag-26machines-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 714}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26machines/26mag-26machines-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 861, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/magazine/26machines/26mag-26machines-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1721, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Learning to Love Our Robot Co-Workers', 'kicker': 'The Future of Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Automation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Layoffs and Job Reductions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robots and Robotics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-23T10:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Tingley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tingley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/516c48d9-dd0c-5486-9aa4-e99c303ecb10', 'word_count': 4612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/516c48d9-dd0c-5486-9aa4-e99c303ecb10'}, {'abstract': 'How does writing distract, calm, energize, entertain, educate and even heal us?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/23/learning/what-purpose-does-writing-serve-in-your-life.html', 'snippet': 'How does writing distract, calm, energize, entertain, educate and even heal us?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do you like to write in a journal, or a blog? Do you have a play or a novel in the making tucked away in a drawer somewhere? Do you like to write poems, stories or songs — or to simply make lists?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/16/world/16XP-Churchill/16XP-Churchill-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 818, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/16/world/16XP-Churchill/16XP-Churchill-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 818}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/16/world/16XP-Churchill/16XP-Churc