ank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emphysema', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-23T18:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Eat', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roni Caryn Rabin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roni', 'middlename': 'Caryn', 'lastname': 'Rabin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffb34578-3afe-573e-af0d-7af2e5d029c1', 'word_count': 213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffb34578-3afe-573e-af0d-7af2e5d029c1'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s top chefs are reinventing the complex sauce — 10, 20, even 30 ingredients at a time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/23/t-magazine/food/mole-mexico-chefs.html', 'snippet': 'The country’s top chefs are reinventing the complex sauce — 10, 20, even 30 ingredients at a time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The word “mole” doesn’t translate into “infinity,” but it feels like it should. The blurring, interlocking, continent-hopping ingredients reflect all of the cultures that have collided to form what Mexico is: the indigenous people who occupied the land first, the European invaders who forced their way in, immigrants from the Middle East and Asia. Often carelessly reduced to simply a sauce, “mole” is, in fact, a word used to unite a complex congregation of sauces — so many varieties with so many ingredients in so many household interpretations across Mexico, that it’s hard to know how to count them all. “In the kitchen, there is no recipe,” says Edgar Nuñez, the chef at Sud 777 in Mexico City. “It’s more a heart thing than theoretical.” Studying mole is like studying the universe: The quest goes on and on.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '90', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/23/t-magazine/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/23/t-magazine/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/23/t-magazine/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/23/t-magazine/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 825, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/23/t-magazine/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 825}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/23/t-magazine/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 746, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/23/t-magazine/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8/mexico-mole-slide-DWS8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1491, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The New Age of Traditional Mexican Mole', 'kicker': 'Wanderlust', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mole in Mexico', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mexican Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-23T18:18:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'subsection_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2cb25a5-29e5-5519-885f-749a46447613', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2cb25a5-29e5-5519-885f-749a46447613'}, {'abstract': 'Also, a friend with boundary issues and an aunt-mother conflict threatens to disrupt a wedding.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/23/style/friend-has-no-boundaries-family-wedding-vacation-etiquette.html', 'snippet': 'Also, a friend with boundary issues and an aunt-mother conflict threatens to disrupt a wedding.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My young cousin was recently married. She and her new husband invited my boyfriend and me to dinner. They used all their new wedding china. (It was a little pretentious in a studio apartment.) During the meal, my boyfriend accidentally broke his wineglass. He may have said, “Let me replace it.” But my cousin didn’t respond; she was sweeping up the glass. Yesterday, I received an email from her with a little note and a receipt for a new wineglass. Tacky?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im