 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbae55e8-f57f-524d-9f52-057bdfa37888', 'word_count': 1035, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbae55e8-f57f-524d-9f52-057bdfa37888'}, {'abstract': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The Times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/23/books/review/8-new-books-we-recommend-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'Suggested reading from critics and editors at The Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a period of relentless news, it’s a relief to come across thought-provoking analysis that transcends the 24/7 cycle to illuminate the present. Two of our recommended nonfiction works this week, Richard Haass’s “A World in Disarray” and Pankaj Mishra’s “The Age of Anger,” fit that bill admirably. Of course, if what you hunger for is escape from anger and disarray, you can always get lost in a novel: this week’s fiction will take you from a railroad in Kenya to a video store (remember video stores?) in Iowa to a mysterious disappearance in Greece.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26ed-one/26ed-one-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26ed-one/26ed-one-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26ed-one/26ed-one-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26ed-one/26ed-one-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 220, 'width': 