ajor': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zwirner, David, Gallery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neel, Alice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-23T22:55:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/653ebbb4-52e5-573e-95e8-f80be055ef7e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/653ebbb4-52e5-573e-95e8-f80be055ef7e'}, {'abstract': 'Two dozen of Neel’s portraits, at David Zwirner, concentrates on her relationships with fellow Harlemites, most of them black, Latin American or Asian.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/23/arts/design/alice-neel-harlem.html', 'snippet': 'Two dozen of Neel’s portraits, at David Zwirner, concentrates on her relationships with fellow Harlemites, most of them black, Latin American or Asian.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I love you Harlem,” the American painter Alice Neel wrote in her diary around the end of World War II, and really, she loved everything in it. Neel celebrated Harlem — specifically its ethnically mixed section known as Spanish Harlem or El Barrio — for “your poverty and your loves.” And what Neel eulogized in her diary, she immortalized in oils: street scenes, interiors and, above all, portraits of the men, women and children in a neighborhood far from the suburban Philadelphia of her youth, which the artist adopted as her own.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/24/arts/24NEEL1/24NEEL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/24/arts/24NEEL1/24NEEL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank':