velt, Franklin Delano', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-24T16:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metropolitan', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d14e591-921f-5f4c-95fa-85fe4c0fd8a9', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d14e591-921f-5f4c-95fa-85fe4c0fd8a9'}, {'abstract': 'Tickling as a sport, with clothed young men tied to a bed? Wholesome frolicking this is not, and it’s on HBO.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/24/arts/television/in-tickled-on-hbo-a-wild-rabbit-hole-of-hunks-and-feathers.html', 'snippet': 'Tickling as a sport, with clothed young men tied to a bed? Wholesome frolicking this is not, and it’s on HBO.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tickling as a pastime and even a fetish, sure. But as a sport? David Farrier, a New Zealand journalist with a lust for the weird and bizarre, knew he had a story when he happened upon an online video for competitive endurance tickling, in which hunky young men were tied to a bed with their clothes on and tickled — and paid for it by a company called Jane O’Brien Media.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE6/26GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE6/26GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE6/26GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE6/26GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1336, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE6/26GUIDE6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE6/26GUIDE6-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE6/26GUIDE6-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In ‘Tickled,’ on HBO, a Wild Rabbit Hole of Hunks and Feathers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Television; Wanted: Hunks.  Must Like Tickling.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tickling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Farrier, David (1982- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reeve, Dylan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-24T16:01:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3ccb9c0-4d85-5f76-b745-55ce6bad8822', 'word_count': 170, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3ccb9c0-4d85-5f76-b745-55ce6bad8822'}, {'abstract': 'A first New York show for Hassan Massoudy, at Sundaram Tagore Chelsea: “Words, Breath, Gesture.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/24/arts/design/an-iraqi-born-artist-who-speaks-through-arcs-of-calligraphy.html', 'snippet': 'A first New York show for Hassan Massoudy, at Sundaram Tagore Chelsea: “Words, Breath, Gesture.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Iraqi-born artist Hassan Massoudy, who is based in Paris, draws on his classical training in calligraphy to create vibrantly colored oversized letters evocative of traditional Arabic script. Having formerly produced headlines for Arabic magazines as a student, Mr. Massoudy now bases his compositions on texts from Eastern and Western poets, authors and philosophers. And at a time of uncertainty for immigrant artists, he is having his first New York solo exhibition, at Sundaram Tagore Chelsea. The show, “Words, Breath, Gesture,” features more than 25 works on paper as well as several paintings on canvas that Mr. Massoudy has created with his own tools and inks. (Through March 25; sundaramtagore.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE3/26GUIDE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Iraqi-Born Artist Who Speaks Through Arcs of Calligraphy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art; Speaking Through Vibrant Arcs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Words, Breath, Gesture (Exhibit)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Massoudy, Hassan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sundaram Tagore Gallery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-24T16:04:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/720b00aa-eec0-5432-a3c5-d79b156fb90b', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/720b00aa-eec0-5432-a3c5-d79b156fb90b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Idiocracy,” part of the series, a G.I. awakens in 2505, when narcotized couch potatoes sit in armchairs with built-in toilet seats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/24/movies/series-of-star-spangled-satires-at-ifc-amid-a-nascent-presidency.html', 'snippet': 'In “Idiocracy,” part of the series, a G.I. awakens in 2505, when narcotized couch potatoes sit in armchairs with built-in toilet seats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If ever an era was in dire need of pointed commentary and point-blank humor, it is our fractious present. So hail the IFC Center for the series Autocratic for the People: An Unpresidented Series of Star-Spangled Satires. And for Mike Judge’s crude but prescient “Idiocracy” (2006), playing Friday, March 3, through Sunday, which nails our media-addled-and-addicted culture with a precision often inducing queasiness.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/arts/26GUIDE2/26GUIDE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '