 to Madison Square Garden on Thursday night, is a show of confidence, prowess and aplomb. “Nothing to prove and/I’m bulletproof and/know what I’m doing,” she sang in “Dangerous Woman,” the title song of her 2016 album and the concert’s bluesy finale. The concert was full of songs about a young woman’s needs and prerogatives, mostly lusty ones, delivered with peppy choruses and without shock value or shame. Ms. Grande often sings about the overwhelming strength of temptation and how much pleasure there is in giving in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/25/arts/25GRANDE/25GRANDE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/25/arts/25GRANDE/25GRANDE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/25/arts/25GRANDE/25GRANDE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/25/arts/25GRANDE/25GRANDE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/25/arts/25GRANDE/25GRANDE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/25/arts/25GRANDE/25GRANDE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/25/arts/25GRANDE/25GRANDE-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Ariana Grande Asserts Herself With Stilettos and a Soaring Voice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop and Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Madison Square Garden', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grande, Ariana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-24T16:46:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a76feb36-882a-58d8-965f-0fafbea3efdb', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a76feb36-882a-58d8-965f-0fafbea3efdb'}, {'abstract': 'The novelist Marisa Silver on how notions of plot have changed through literature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/24/books/review/what-happens-next-or-doesnt.html', 'snippet': 'The novelist Marisa Silver on how notions of plot have changed through literature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There were things my husband brought to the marriage that had to be gotten rid of. The grandfatherly forest green recliner. Certain blocky-looking shirts bought for him by his mother. The occasional phone call from an old girlfriend. There was one item that was a keeper, though, a volume called “Plotto: The Master Book of All Plots.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/0226-BKS-CriticsTake/0226-BKS-CriticsTake-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subTyp