e, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/25/us/25jenner_xp/25jenner_xp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/25/us/25jenner_xp/25jenner_xp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1290, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Caitlyn Jenner Slams Trump on Transgender Order: ‘See You in Court’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Caitlyn Jenner Deplores Transgender Order', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jenner, Caitlyn (1949- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexuals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathrooms and Toilets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-24T16:54:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Haag', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haag', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38442d6c-768a-52fd-a4e8-a843c3310c13', 'word_count': 457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38442d6c-768a-52fd-a4e8-a843c3310c13'}, {'abstract': '“Lincoln in the Bardo,” about the president mourning his young son, goes straight to the top of the hardcover fiction list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/24/books/review/george-saunders-famous-for-futuristic-short-stories-hits-no-1-with-a-historical-novel.html', 'snippet': '“Lincoln in the Bardo,” about the president mourning his young son, goes straight to the top of the hardcover fiction list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mourning in America: Could Joe Biden have been the next Abraham Lincoln? That thought came to mind recently thanks to George Saunders’s debut novel, “Lincoln in the Bardo” (new at No.\u202f1 in hardcover fiction), which centers on Lincoln’s sorrow over the death of his young son. Biden, you may remember, opted not to run for president shortly after his son Beau died of cancer in 2015 — a wrenching and understandable decision, of course, but one cast in retrospective relief by the suggestion in Saunders’s novel that private grief made Lincoln a better public servant. “What moved me about Lincoln’s arc during his presidency,” Saunders told me via email, “was the way that the burdens of the office — the floundering war effort, intense public criticism, the mistakes he made that were costing so many lives, the death of his son — beat him down and made him sorrowful, but also, almost causally, seemed to expand the reach of his empathy, so that, by the end, it included soldiers on both sides and the millions of Americans being enslaved by other Americans. It seemed to me that the empathy was somehow a byproduct of the sorrow — a burning-away of his hopes and dreams that resulted in a kind of naked seeing of things as they really were. For me, the book was about that terrible conundrum: We seem to be born to love, but everything we love comes to an end. What do we do with that? How can we keep going and live positive lives under that shadow? I came to understand Lincoln as someone so beat down by sadness and loss that he developed a sort of crazy wisdom — as if, in sadness, all of the comforting bromides that normally keep us from the harsher truths were denied him. Empathy might even thrive best in this state, where the easy comforts are denied us. Conversely, empathy doesn’t do well in a climate of fear or anxiety, when one forgets the particular and individual and, in a panic, begins theorizing about whole groups of people without knowing many of them.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26list-saunders/26list-saunders-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26list-saunders/26list-saunders-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26list-saunders/26list-saunders-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26list-saunders/26list-saunders-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 268, 'width': 260, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/books/review/26list-saunders/26list-saunders-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 268, 'width': 260, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'George Saunders, Famous for Futuristic Short Stories, Hits No. 1 With a Historical Novel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saunders, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Lincoln in the Bardo (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-24T16:56:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61bafee3-6bc8-564e-a3cf-35029e3228ed', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61bafee3-6bc8-564e-a3cf-35029e3228ed'}, {'abstract': 'What’s essential to Dave Malloy, the composer of “Natasha, Pierre & the Great Comet of 1812,” and his wife, the writer Eliza Bent? Stuffed animals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/24/realestate/in-a-house-of-whimsy-room-for-mischief.html', 'snippet': 'What’s essential to Dave Malloy, the composer of “Natasha, Pierre & the Great Comet of 1812,” and his wife, the writer Eliza Bent? Stuffed animals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dave Malloy, the composer and lyricist of the Broadway musical “Natasha, Pierre & the Great Comet of 1812,” lives in a one-bedroom rental in Park Slope, Brooklyn, with large windows, high ceilings, a small office, two fireplaces (they don’t work, but are charming nonetheless) and many toys and stuffed animals. Those expecting an embarrassed disclaimer about the playthings will be disappointed.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/26/realestate/26WHATILOVE5/26WHATILOVE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/26/realestate/26WHATILOVE5/26WHATILOVE5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'