fb'}, {'abstract': 'The intentional walk never lived up to the artistry of baseball.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/28/opinion/speed-walking-baseball-in-a-hurry.html', 'snippet': 'The intentional walk never lived up to the artistry of baseball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Major League Baseball and the Players Association agreed last week to change the intentional walk protocol so that the pitcher will no longer actually have to throw the four pitches that had been required. The reason? After some progress in speeding up the “pace of play,” baseball experienced a regression in 2016, as the average length of a game increased by four minutes. Under this new rule, the manager of the pitching team will simply point and the umpire will then award first base to the hitter. No mess.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28glanvilleWeb/28glanvilleWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28glanvilleWeb/28glanvilleWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28glanvilleWeb/28glanvilleWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28glanvilleWeb/28glanvilleWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28glanvilleWeb/28glanvilleWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28glanvilleWeb/28glanvilleWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28glanvilleWeb/28glanvilleWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1288, 'width': 1936, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Speed Walking: Baseball in a Hurry', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bonds, Barry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramirez, Manny', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-28T08:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doug Glanville', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doug', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glanville', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/08966db9-946b-5cdf-9f2e-c75517a705ca', 'word_count': 1182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08966db9-946b-5cdf-9f2e-c75517a705ca'}, {'abstract': 'Soldiers really did read it for the articles — and for the chance to see the war for what it really was.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2017/02/28/opinion/how-playboy-explains-vietnam.html', 'snippet': 'Soldiers really did read it for the articles — and for the chance to see the war for what it really was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a famous scene about halfway through “Apocalypse Now” in which Martin Sheen’s river boat pulls into a supply base, deep in the jungle. While the crew members are buying diesel fuel, the supply clerk gives them free tickets to a show — “You know,” he says, “the bunnies.” Soon they’re sitting in an improvised amphitheater around a landing pad, watching as three Playboy models hop out of a helicopter and dance to “Suzie Q.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28baturaWeb/28baturaWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28baturaWeb/28baturaWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28baturaWeb/28baturaWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28baturaWeb/28baturaWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28baturaWeb/28baturaWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28baturaWeb/28baturaWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2017/02/28/opinion/28baturaWeb/28baturaWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1353, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Playboy Explains Vietnam', 'kicker': "Vietnam '67", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Playboy Magazine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hefner, Hugh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'APOCALYPSE NOW (MOVIE)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2017-02-28T08:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amber Batura', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amber', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Batura', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/74894d19-e767-52ed-988a-59ae87bff4c2', 'word_count': 1540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74894d19-e767-52ed-988a-59ae87bff4c2'}, {'abstract': 'Jost Franko photographed the global path of cotton — from plantation to factory — where the corporate quest for cheaper production sometimes obscures the human cost.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2017/02/28/a-glimpse-of-the-workers-who-make-your-clothes/', 'snippet': 'Jost Franko photographed the global path of cotton — from plantation to factory — where the corporate quest for cheaper production sometimes obscures the human cost.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After photographing social inequality in his native Slovenia and internationally, Jost Franko has concluded that “profit over people” is the ideology that propels some of the world’s most profitable businesses. With his latest project, “Cotton Black, Cotton Blue,” he examined a global industry that from plantation to factory can be merciless and grueling for its workforce, which can even include children.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Glimpse of the Workers Who Make Your Clothes', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'v